  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2014-p13.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Follow the \\(60^\\circ\\) grid formed by the unit hexagons and place \\(A=(0,0)\\). The marked vertices may then be written as \\(B=(3,\\sqrt3)\\) and \\(C=(3,-\\sqrt3)\\).\n\nThus \\(BC=2\\sqrt3\\), and \\[ \\begin{aligned} AB=AC&=\\sqrt{3^2+(\\sqrt3)^2}\\\\ &=2\\sqrt3. \\end{aligned} \\] Hence \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is equilateral, with area \\[ \\frac{(2\\sqrt3)^2\\sqrt3}{4}=3\\sqrt3. \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is B .",
      "hints": [
        "Use the regular hexagon angles to find the side of \\(\\triangle ABC\\)",
        "The resulting triangle is equilateral"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
